class Solution {
/*
Overlapping Condition:
For two intervals [a, b] and [c, d] where a < b and c < d, the meetings overlap if and only if:

a < d
b >= c

So the crux is to maximize the number of non-overlapping intervals/meetings
to accommodate the maximum number of meetings in a single room — 
which will give us the answer.

So if there are non-overlapping intervals, we will keep increasing the count.

And if we encounter an overlapping interval,
we will keep the interval/meeting that is having the smaller end time
i.e., skip the one with larger end time...

The reason is because the larger end time means there is a greater possibility 
that more overlapping intervals would be encountered
if we would have kept the meeting with the larger end time.


Dry Run for:
start[] = [1, 4, 7, 10, 11, 12, 13, 14, 17, 20]
end[]   = [3, 6, 9, 14, 15, 13, 12, 15, 18, 22]

Meetings after pairing:
[ [1,3], [4,6], [7,9], [10,14], [11,15], [12,13], [13,12], [14,15], [17,18], [20,22] ]

Sorted by start time:
[ [1,3], [4,6], [7,9], [10,14], [11,15], [12,13], [13,12], [14,15], [17,18], [20,22] ]

Initialize:
count = 1
lastMeeting = [1,3]

Step-by-step:

1. Meeting [4,6]
4 > 3 → No overlap → count = 2
✅ Last meeting = [4,6]

2. Meeting [7,9]
7 > 6 → No overlap → count = 3
✅ Last meeting = [7,9]

3. Meeting [10,14]
10 > 9 → No overlap → count = 4
✅ Last meeting = [10,14]


4. Meeting [11,15]
11 < 14 → Overlap  
currentMeetingEnd (15) > lastMeetingEnd (14) → ❌ Skip current meeting[11,15]  
✅ Last meeting = [10,14]
count = 4

5. Meeting [12,13]
12 < 14 → Overlap  
currentMeetingEnd (13) < lastMeetingEnd (14) → ✅ Keep current meeting [ 12,13], drop last meeting   
✅ Last meeting = [12,13]

6. Meeting [13,12]
13 == 13 → Overlap  
currentMeetingEnd (12) < lastMeetingEnd (13) → ✅ Keep current meeting [13,12], drop last meeting   
✅ Last meeting = [13,12]
count = 4

7. Meeting [14,15]
14 > 12 → No overlap → count = 5  
✅ Last meeting = [14,15]


8. Meeting [17,18]
17 > 15 → No overlap → count = 6  
✅ Last meeting = [17,18]

9. Meeting [20,22]
20 > 18 → No overlap → count = 7  
✅ Last meeting = [20,22]

✅ Final Output: 7 meetings can be scheduled:
[1,3], [4,6], [7,9], [13,12], [14,15], [17,18], [20,22]
*/

    public int maxMeetings(int[] start, int[] end) {
        int[][] meetings = new int[start.length][2];

        // Step 1: Pair start and end times
        for (int i = 0; i < start.length; i++) {
            meetings[i] = new int[]{start[i], end[i]};
        }

        // Step 2: Sort meetings based on start time
        Arrays.sort(meetings, (m1, m2) -> Integer.compare(m1[0], m2[0]));

        int lastMeetingIndex = 0;
        int currentMeetingIndex = 1;
        int count = 1;

        // Step 3: Traverse remaining meetings
        while (currentMeetingIndex < meetings.length) {
            int currentMeetingStart = meetings[currentMeetingIndex][0];
            int currentMeetingEnd = meetings[currentMeetingIndex][1];
            int lastMeetingEnd= meetings[lastMeetingIndex][1];

            // Case 1: No overlap
            if (currentMeetingStart > lastMeetingEnd) {
                count++;
                // Schedule current meeting: update last meeting and move to next meeting
                lastMeetingIndex = currentMeetingIndex;
                currentMeetingIndex++;
            } else {
                // Case 2: Overlap detected
                if (currentMeetingEnd >= lastMeetingEnd) {
                    // Skip current meeting and keep the last one since it ends earlier than or equal to current
                    currentMeetingIndex++;
                } else {
                    // Skip last meeting and keep current one since it ends earlier, reducing future overlaps
                    lastMeetingIndex = currentMeetingIndex;
                    currentMeetingIndex++;
                }
            }
        }

        return count;
    }
}
